.TH TIME 1 "March 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
time \- time a simple command or give resource usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B time
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-c | \-t ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR fmt ]
.RI [ utility\ [argument\ ...] ]
.br
.B time
.RB [ \-\-portability ]
.RB [ \-\-output=\fIfile\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-append ]
.RB [ \-\-format=\fIfmt\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-monotonic ]
.RB [ \-\-realtime ]
.RB [ \-\-compat=\fIsys\fP ]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B time
utility runs the specified program
.I utility
with the given arguments. When
.I utility
finishes,
.B time
writes a message to standard error giving timing statistics about this program run.
.PP
These statistics consist of (i) the elapsed real time between invocation and
termination, (ii) the user CPU time, and (iii) the system CPU time.
.PP
If no utility is specified,
.B time
prints a usage message and exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-portability
Print the timing results in the POSIX-specified multi-line format.
.TP
.B \-h
Print times in a human-readable format (e.g. 1m2.34s or hours/minutes). This is a BSD extension.
.TP
.B \-l
Print the contents of the rusage structure as well. This is a BSD extension.
.TP
.BR \-o " " \fIfile\fP ", " \-\-output=\fIfile\fP
Write the resource use statistics to
.I file
instead of to the standard error stream. By default, this overwrites the file.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-append
Append the resource use information to the output file instead of overwriting it.
This option is only useful with the
.B \-o
or
.B \-\-output
option.
.TP
.B \-c
Use the csh-style default output format.
.TP
.B \-t
Use the tcsh-style default output format.
.TP
.BR \-f " " \fIfmt\fP ", " \-\-format=\fIfmt\fP
Use the specified format string to control the output.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output the gathered statistics as a structured JSON object.
.TP
.B \-\-monotonic
Explicitly select the monotonic clock for accurate duration tracking (the default).
.TP
.B \-\-realtime
Explicitly select the realtime (wall-clock) source for duration tracking.
.TP
.B \-\-compat=\fIsys\fP
Enable bug-for-bug compatibility with explicit legacy behaviors (e.g., \fIfreebsd\fP, \fIgnu\fP).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TIME
If this variable is set, its value is used as the format string providing no
other format override was specified using
.B \-f
or
.B \-\-format
.SH NOTES
Where BSD and GNU extensions conflict (for instance, the meaning of the \-h flag),
the BSD semantics take precedence. The \-h flag provides human readable output,
not help information. Use \-\-help for usage info.
.SH EXIT STATUS
If
.I utility
could be invoked, the exit status is the exit status of
.I utility .
Otherwise, the following exit values are returned:
.TP
.B 126
.I utility
was found, but could not be invoked.
.TP
.B 127
.I utility
could not be found.
.TP
.B 1\-125
An error occurred in the
.B time
utility itself.
.SH CONFORMING TO
POSIX.1-2024 Shell and Utilities specification, with BSD and GNU extensions.
